const e=[{id:"sv-2-161",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A team agrees the effect *“Business-broadband faults take longer than 4 hours to restore.”* Its eight members have dozens of ideas about why. The Black Belt wants to capture **every possible cause** and group the ideas into logical categories before any data are collected. Which tool should the team use **next**?",options:["A cause-and-effect (fishbone) diagram","A Pareto chart of fault categories","A scatter plot of restore time against technician experience","An I-MR control chart of daily restore time"],answer:0,hint:"Generate and group *potential* causes of one effect → fishbone (cause-and-effect) diagram. Ranking and testing with data come later.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The fishbone diagram is a structured brainstorming tool: the effect sits at the head and each bone (for example the 6Ms or 4Ps) prompts the team for causes in that area."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The team has an agreed effect, many opinions and **no data yet**, and wants every idea captured and grouped. That is exactly the job of a fishbone."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Pareto, scatter plot and control chart all need data and answer different questions (which category is biggest, does X relate to Y, is the process stable)."}],final:"Brainstorm and group all possible causes → fishbone → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A Pareto chart ranks categories that have already been counted; it does not generate or organise possible causes.","A scatter plot tests one suspected X against Y with data; it comes after the causes have been listed and narrowed down.","A control chart shows whether the process is stable over time; it does not list causes."],bookRef:"Book pp. 98, 113–114, 118"},{id:"sv-2-162",phase:2,topic:"2.1.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Which order is correct for building a fishbone diagram in a team workshop?

- **P** Classify each cause as controllable, noise or procedural and choose which to verify with data
- **Q** Write the agreed effect (problem statement) at the head of the diagram
- **R** Brainstorm causes onto each bone, asking “why?” to add sub-causes
- **S** Draw the main category bones (for example the 6Ms or 4Ps)`,options:["Q → R → P → S","Q → S → P → R","Q → S → R → P","S → Q → R → P"],answer:2,hint:"Effect first → category bones → causes and sub-causes (“why?”) → classify C/N/P → pick causes to verify.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A fishbone works from the **effect** backwards: the head states the problem, the bones give categories, and the team fills the bones with causes and sub-causes."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So: write the effect (Q) → draw the category bones (S) → brainstorm causes and ask “why?” (R) → classify each cause as C, N or P and choose which to check with data (P)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Any order that brainstorms before the bones exist, or classifies before there are causes, breaks the logic."}],final:"Q → S → R → P → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The category bones are the prompts for brainstorming; drawing them last means the ideas were not organised as they were generated.","Causes cannot be classified as C, N or P before they have been brainstormed.","","The bones are chosen to suit the effect, so the effect must be agreed and written first."],bookRef:"Book pp. 113–114, 118"},{id:"sv-2-163",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A value stream map of a SIM-replacement process shows three sequential steps with these first-pass yields: **Order capture 95%**, **Identity check 90%** and **Dispatch 98%**. What is the **rolled throughput yield (RTY)** of the process?",options:["94.3%","83.8%","90.0%","83.0%"],answer:1,hint:"$$RTY=Y_1\\times Y_2\\times \\dots \\times Y_n$$ (first-pass yield of each step, multiplied)",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Step yields: $Y_1=0.95$, $Y_2=0.90$, $Y_3=0.98$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$RTY=Y_1\\times Y_2\\times Y_3$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$RTY=0.95\\times 0.90\\times 0.98=0.8379$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About **83.8%** of SIM replacements pass all three steps right first time."}],final:"RTY = 83.8% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This is the average of the three yields; RTY multiplies the step yields because a unit must pass every step first time.","","This is only the lowest single-step yield; the other two steps also lose units.","This subtracts the summed defect rates (5% + 10% + 2%) from 100%; yields must be multiplied, not their losses added."],bookRef:"Book p. 105"},{id:"sv-2-164",phase:2,topic:"2.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"The sponsor has approved a high-level map of the number-porting process with six boxes. The Black Belt now needs a map of **one of those steps** showing every task and decision, with cycle times, defect counts and which tasks are value-added, to pinpoint where to improve. Which kind of process map is this?",options:["A SIPOC diagram of the porting process","Level 1 – macro (management-level) process map","Level 2 – worker-level process map without performance data","Level 3 – micro (improvement-level) process map"],answer:3,hint:"Level 1 = macro (management) · Level 2 = worker steps · Level 3 = micro (improvement) with performance data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book uses three levels of process map, like road maps from country to street: Level 1 macro, Level 2 worker level, Level 3 micro or improvement level."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Tasks **plus** cycle time, defects and value-added classification for one step is the Level 3 (micro) map."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Level 1 and SIPOC are too high-level; Level 2 lacks the performance data the Black Belt asked for."}],final:"Task-level map with performance data → Level 3 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A SIPOC is a high-level scoping tool (suppliers to customers); it shows no task-level detail or performance data.","Level 1 is the 4–8 box management view the team already has.","Level 2 shows the worker’s steps and decisions, but the performance data (yields, cycle time, value-added and non-value-added time, defects) are added at Level 3.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 102"},{id:"sv-2-165",phase:2,topic:"2.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"After a fishbone session a contact-centre team has **28 controllable inputs** and three customer outputs (first-contact resolution, handle time and CSAT), which the sponsor has weighted from 1 to 10. The team wants to use its process knowledge to cut the inputs down to a short list worth collecting data on. Which tool fits?",options:["A Pareto chart of complaint categories","An FMEA that rates severity, occurrence and detection for each input","A hypothesis test of each of the 28 inputs against CSAT","An X-Y diagram (matrix) that scores each input against the weighted outputs"],answer:3,hint:"Controllable X’s from the fishbone + weighted Y’s → X-Y diagram → rank the inputs by total score.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The X-Y diagram lists the X’s in rows and the weighted Y’s in columns. The team scores how strongly each X affects each Y, and the weighted totals rank the inputs."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Many controllable inputs, several weighted outputs and a need to narrow the list using team knowledge → X-Y diagram."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The FMEA, Pareto chart and hypothesis tests answer different questions or need data the team does not yet have."}],final:"Rank controllable X’s against weighted Y’s → X-Y diagram → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A Pareto chart ranks counted categories; it does not relate inputs to several weighted outputs.","The FMEA rates the risk of failure modes; the book sends the procedural and noise inputs there, while controllable inputs are ranked in the X-Y diagram.","Testing every input is Analyze work and needs data for all 28; the X-Y diagram exists to decide which few are worth that effort.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 119–122"},{id:"sv-2-166",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:`Two failure modes in a billing FMEA have the **same RPN of 120**:

- Failure mode 1: S = 10, O = 3, D = 4
- Failure mode 2: S = 4, O = 6, D = 5

The team can work on only one of them this month. What is the usual choice?`,options:["Failure mode 2, because it occurs more often","Neither, because only failure modes with an RPN above 200 need action","Either one, because equal RPNs mean equal risk","Failure mode 1, because an effect with severity 10 is given priority even when the RPNs are equal"],answer:3,hint:"RPN = S × O × D ranks the risks; when RPNs are close or equal, act first on the higher severity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The RPN multiplies three ratings, so 10 × 3 × 4 and 4 × 6 × 5 both give 120 even though the risks are different."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Failure mode 1 has a severity of 10: when it happens, the effect on the customer is the worst possible. Common FMEA practice gives such effects priority."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Equal RPN does not mean equal risk, and there is no fixed RPN threshold below which action is unnecessary."}],final:"Equal RPNs → act on the severity-10 failure mode → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Occurrence matters, but a severity of 10 (serious harm or loss to the customer) normally takes priority when the RPNs tie.","There is no universal RPN cut-off; teams set their own, and a severity of 9 or 10 is acted on whatever the RPN.","The RPN is a product, so the same number can hide very different risks; the ratings behind it must be read.",""],trap:"Never treat equal RPNs as equal risks: read the S, O and D behind the product.",bookRef:"Book pp. 129–134"},{id:"sv-2-167",phase:2,topic:"2.1.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"In an FMEA, a **higher** detection rating (for example 9 or 10) means the current controls are **more likely** to catch the failure before it reaches the customer. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Detection: 1 = almost certain to be detected · 10 = almost impossible to detect. High numbers are always bad in an FMEA.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"All three FMEA scales are built so that a **high** number means **high risk**. For detection, 10 means the controls will almost never catch the failure, and 1 means they are almost certain to."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A rating of 9 or 10 therefore means **poor** detection, the opposite of the statement."}],final:"High detection rating = poor detection → **False** → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The scale runs the other way: 10 means detection is almost impossible and 1 means it is almost certain.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 133–134"},{id:"sv-2-168",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt’s data collection plan lists these measures:

| Measure | Data type recorded in the plan |
|---|---|
| Time to restore service (minutes) | Continuous |
| Number of repeat calls per customer in 30 days | Continuous |
| Customer segment (Consumer, SME, Enterprise) | Attribute (nominal) |
| Survey answer: Poor / Fair / Good / Excellent | Attribute (ordinal) |

Which row is classified **incorrectly**?`,options:["Time to restore service: it is discrete because it is reported in whole minutes","Number of repeat calls per customer: it is a count, so it is discrete, not continuous","Survey answer: it should be continuous because the answers can be coded 1–4 and averaged","Customer segment: it should be ordinal because Enterprise customers are larger"],answer:1,hint:"Continuous = measured on a scale (time, weight, speed). Discrete = counted (number of calls). Attribute = categories (nominal or ordinal).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Counts of events can only take whole values (0, 1, 2 …), so they are discrete, even though they are numbers."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Repeat calls **per customer** is a count, so “Continuous” is the wrong label. The other three rows are classified correctly."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Rounding does not turn time into counts, segment names are nominal, and survey categories are ordinal however they are coded."}],final:"Repeat-call count is discrete → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Time is continuous by nature; rounding to whole minutes reduces resolution but does not change the data type.","","Coding ordered categories as numbers does not make the gaps between them equal; the data stay ordinal.","Segments are names of groups. Ranking them by size is not the purpose of the label, so nominal is correct."],bookRef:"Book pp. 142–145"},{id:"sv-2-169",phase:2,topic:"2.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"To estimate typical daily call volume over a year, an analyst takes a **systematic sample**: starting on a Monday, every **7th day** is selected. Call volume has a strong weekly pattern (busy Mondays, quiet Sundays). What is the main problem with this sample?",options:["Systematic sampling turns continuous data into attribute data","The sample is too small, because about 52 days cannot describe a whole year","Every sampled day falls on the same weekday, so the sampling interval matches the weekly cycle and the estimate is biased","Systematic samples are never valid; only simple random samples can be used"],answer:2,hint:"Systematic sampling (every k-th item) is fine unless k matches a cycle in the process; then every sample sits at the same point of the cycle.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A sample must be **representative**. Systematic sampling picks every k-th unit, which is simple and spreads the sample over time."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Here k = 7 matches the weekly cycle, so every sampled day is a Monday, the busiest day. The yearly average will be overstated."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The fix is to change the interval or use random or stratified sampling (for example by weekday), not to abandon sampling or simply take more Mondays."}],final:"Interval matches the weekly cycle → biased sample → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The sampling method does not change the data type; call volume stays a count.","About 52 values is a reasonable size for estimating a mean; the problem is bias, which a bigger sample with the same interval would not fix.","","Systematic sampling is valid and practical when the interval does not line up with a pattern in the data."],bookRef:"Book p. 141; Syllabus 2.2.1"},{id:"sv-2-170",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab output for complaint closure times:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: ClosureTime (days)

Variable      N   Mean  StDev  Minimum     Q1  Median     Q3  Maximum  Skewness
ClosureTime  90  18.62  14.87     2.10   8.40   12.10  24.30    71.50      1.94
\`\`\`

Which statistic best describes the **typical** closure time for the steering committee?`,options:["The median, 12.10 days, because the data are strongly right-skewed and the mean is pulled up by a few very long cases","The standard deviation, 14.87 days, because it describes the process","The mean, 18.62 days, because it uses every data point","Q3, 24.30 days, because three-quarters of complaints close by then"],answer:0,hint:"Skewed data → report the median as the centre (and the IQR as the spread). Symmetric data → mean and standard deviation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In a symmetric distribution the mean and median are close. A positive skewness and a mean well above the median show a long right tail."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Skewness = 1.94 and the mean (18.62) is more than 6 days above the median (12.10), so the median is the better “typical” value."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The standard deviation and Q3 do not describe the centre at all."}],final:"Right-skewed data → median 12.10 days → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The standard deviation measures spread, not the typical (central) value.","The mean uses every value, which is exactly why a long right tail (maximum 71.5 days) drags it above what most customers experience.","Q3 is a position measure for the upper quarter, not a measure of the centre."],bookRef:"Book pp. 147–150"},{id:"sv-2-171",phase:2,topic:"2.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which measure of spread is expressed in the **same units as the data** and describes the typical distance of the values from the mean?",options:["Coefficient of variation","Standard deviation","Variance","Range"],answer:1,hint:"Variance = average squared deviation (squared units); standard deviation = its square root (data units).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The standard deviation is the square root of the variance, so it returns to the original units and can be read as a typical distance from the mean."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Variance is squared, the range depends on only two points, and the coefficient of variation has no units."}],final:"Same units, typical distance from the mean → standard deviation → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The coefficient of variation is the standard deviation divided by the mean, a unit-free ratio.","","The variance is in **squared** units (for example minutes²), which is why its square root is usually reported.","The range is in the data units, but it uses only the two extreme values, not the typical distance from the mean."],bookRef:"Book pp. 147–148"},{id:"sv-2-172",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"Round-trip latency on a core link is normally distributed with mean **40 ms** and standard deviation **2.5 ms**. What is the **Z-score** of a reading of **46 ms**?",options:["2.40","18.40","0.42","6.00"],answer:0,hint:"$$Z=\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$x=46$ ms, $\\mu=40$ ms, $\\sigma=2.5$ ms."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$Z=\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$Z=\\dfrac{46-40}{2.5}=\\dfrac{6}{2.5}=2.40$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The reading lies **2.4 standard deviations** above the mean."}],final:"Z = 2.40 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This divides the reading itself by σ (46 ÷ 2.5) without first subtracting the mean.","This divides σ by the difference (2.5 ÷ 6), turning the formula upside down.","This is only the difference from the mean (46 − 40); it must be divided by the standard deviation."],bookRef:"Book pp. 152–153"},{id:"sv-2-173",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Before a capability study, handle times from two shifts are tested for normality (α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
Normality Test (Anderson-Darling)

Variable            N   Mean  StDev     AD  P-Value
HandleTime_ShiftA  50   6.84   1.21  0.482    0.223
HandleTime_ShiftB  50   7.95   2.64  0.871    0.024
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["Shift A: reject normality. Shift B: fail to reject normality","Shift A: fail to reject normality. Shift B: reject normality, so its data are significantly non-normal","Both shifts: reject normality, because both AD values are above 0.05","Shift A: the data are proven to be normal. Shift B: reject normality"],answer:1,hint:"H0: data are normal. P < α → reject normality; P ≥ α → fail to reject (treat as normal enough). Never say “accept” or “prove”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For the Anderson-Darling test H0 says the data follow a normal distribution. A P-value below 0.05 rejects H0."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Shift A: P = 0.223 > 0.05 → fail to reject normality. Shift B: P = 0.024 < 0.05 → reject normality."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Watch the wording: “proven normal” and “accept H0” are both wrong, and the AD value is not compared with α."}],final:"A fail to reject, B reject → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This reverses the rule: P < α rejects normality, so it is Shift B (0.024) that is rejected.","","The decision uses the P-value, not the AD statistic; comparing AD with α mixes up the two numbers.","A large P-value does not **prove** normality; we only fail to reject the null hypothesis that the data are normal."],trap:"“Fail to reject normality” is not “proven normal”.",bookRef:"Book pp. 155–157"},{id:"sv-2-174",phase:2,topic:"2.2.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"An Anderson-Darling normality test gives **P-value = 0.30**. This **proves** that the data come from a normal distribution. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"P ≥ α → fail to reject H0 (normal is a reasonable assumption). A test can reject H0 but can never prove it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A hypothesis test can only show evidence **against** H0. When P is large we “fail to reject”, which means normality is a reasonable working assumption, not a proven fact."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"P = 0.30 > 0.05, so we fail to reject normality. The word “proves” makes the statement false."}],final:"Fail to reject ≠ proof → **False** → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A large P-value means there is not enough evidence to reject normality; it never proves that the null hypothesis is true.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 156–157"},{id:"sv-2-175",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A coach has only **12 handle times for each of two new agents** and wants one simple graph that shows **every individual value** for both agents side by side, so that clusters and gaps are visible. Which graph is the best choice?",options:["A Pareto chart of the handle times","A histogram for each agent","A box plot for each agent","A dot plot (individual value plot) of the two agents"],answer:3,hint:"Small samples and “show every point” → dot plot or individual value plot. Large samples and “show the shape” → histogram.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A dot plot places one dot per observation along a scale, so every value stays visible. It works well for small samples and for comparing groups."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Twelve values per agent, compared side by side, with every value visible → dot plot (individual value plot)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Histograms and box plots summarise the data; a Pareto chart is for categories."}],final:"Show every value for small groups → dot plot → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A Pareto chart ranks counts of categories; it is not used for continuous values such as handle time.","With only 12 values a histogram’s bars look lumpy, and the bars hide the individual values.","A box plot summarises the data into quartiles and whiskers, so most individual values are hidden.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 159, 162"},{id:"sv-2-176",phase:2,topic:"2.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A histogram of 400 field-installation times shows **two clear peaks**, one near 90 minutes and one near 150 minutes. The data were pooled from two regions and two installation types. What should the Black Belt do **first**?",options:["Stratify the data (for example by region and by installation type) to see whether two different processes are mixed together","Transform the data until they look normal and carry on","Run a normal capability analysis on all 400 points, because the sample is large","Delete the values in the smaller peak as outliers"],answer:0,hint:"Two peaks (bimodal) usually mean two populations mixed together → stratify by the likely sources before any further analysis.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A bimodal histogram usually means two processes, shifts, regions or product types have been pooled. The book calls this “good news”: the cause is often easy to find."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The data come from two regions and two installation types, so the first step is to split (stratify) them and look at each group separately."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Normal capability, transformation and deleting data all ignore the two populations."}],final:"Bimodal data → stratify first → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Transforming hides the real message of two peaks: two different sources of variation that should be understood.","A large sample does not make mixed data normal; a normal capability study would describe neither process.","A whole cluster of values is not a set of outliers; deleting it throws away half of the story."],bookRef:"Book pp. 149, 166; Book p. 221"},{id:"sv-2-177",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A speed-test app is checked on a reference line that truly delivers **100 Mbps**. Twenty repeat tests read between **91.8 and 92.3 Mbps**. How should this measurement system be described?",options:["Both accurate and precise, because the readings vary by only 0.5 Mbps","Accurate but not precise","Precise but not accurate: the readings repeat closely but are offset from the true value","Neither accurate nor precise"],answer:2,hint:"Accuracy = average close to the true value (no bias). Precision = small spread on repeated measurements.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Accuracy is about **where** the readings are centred compared with the true value; precision is about **how close together** repeated readings are."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The readings are within 0.5 Mbps of each other (precise) but centred about 8 Mbps below the true 100 Mbps (biased, so not accurate)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Picture a target: a tight cluster of shots away from the bull’s-eye."}],final:"Tight cluster, off target → precise but not accurate → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A small spread shows precision only; accuracy also needs the average to be close to the true 100 Mbps.","This is the reverse: the readings are tightly grouped (precise) but about 8 Mbps away from the truth (not accurate).","","The spread of only 0.5 Mbps shows the readings are precise; only accuracy is missing."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 179–181"},{id:"sv-2-178",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"In measurement system analysis, all of the following are components of **precision** (measurement variation) EXCEPT:",options:["Reproducibility","Operator-by-part interaction","Bias","Repeatability"],answer:2,hint:"Precision = Gage R&R = repeatability + reproducibility (operator + operator × part). Accuracy = bias, linearity, stability.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Precision describes the **spread** of measurement error and splits into repeatability and reproducibility; Minitab adds the operator and operator × part terms into reproducibility."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Bias is the difference between the average reading and the true value. It describes **location** (accuracy), not spread."}],final:"Bias is an accuracy term → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Reproducibility (appraiser variation) is the other main part of precision.","The operator-by-part interaction is part of reproducibility, so it is also a precision component.","","Repeatability (equipment variation) is one of the two main parts of precision."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173–174, 179, 184"},{id:"sv-2-179",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:`A call-duration logger is checked against three reference recordings, each measured 20 times:

| Reference (s) | Average reading (s) |
|---|---|
| 60 | 60.1 |
| 300 | 300.5 |
| 600 | 600.9 |

The spread of the readings is similar at every level. Which property of the measurement system is the main concern?`,options:["Repeatability: readings vary when the same reference is measured again","Linearity: the bias changes across the measuring range","Stability: the bias changes over time","Reproducibility: different operators get different averages"],answer:1,hint:"Bias = average reading − reference. Bias that changes with the size of the reference → linearity; bias that changes with time → stability.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Linearity asks whether the bias stays the same across the operating range of the gauge."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Bias = 60.1 − 60 = 0.1 s, then 0.5 s, then 0.9 s. The bias grows as the reference grows, which is a linearity problem."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Stability is about time, while repeatability and reproducibility are about spread and operators, which are not the issue here."}],final:"Bias grows with the reference value → linearity → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The spread is similar and small at every level; the issue is where the averages sit, not how much they vary.","","Stability needs the same reference measured over a period of time; here different references were measured, not different dates.","Nothing in the study compares operators."],bookRef:"Book pp. 181–183"},{id:"sv-2-180",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"A gauge can show **zero bias** at the middle of its range and still have a **linearity** problem. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Linearity = how bias changes across the range. Bias at one point does not tell you the slope.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Linearity is judged by the **slope** of bias against reference value, not by the bias at a single point."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A gauge that reads low at the bottom of its range, correctly in the middle and high at the top has zero bias in the middle but poor linearity. The statement is true."}],final:"Zero mid-range bias can hide a slope → **True** → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Zero bias at one point says nothing about the other points; the bias can be negative at one end and positive at the other."],bookRef:"Book pp. 182–183"},{id:"sv-2-181",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
Gage R&R Study - ANOVA Method: Jitter probe (ms)

                                Study Var  %Study Var
Source              StdDev (SD)  (6 × SD)       (%SV)
Total Gage R&R          0.1230    0.7381       24.61
  Repeatability         0.1050    0.6300       21.00
  Reproducibility       0.0641    0.3846       12.82
Part-To-Part            0.4846    2.9076       96.93
Total Variation         0.5000    3.0000      100.00

Number of Distinct Categories = 5
\`\`\`

Using the usual guidelines, how should this measurement system be judged?`,options:["Unacceptable, because the %Study Var values add up to more than 100%","Marginal: it may be acceptable depending on the importance of the measurement and the cost of improving it, and ndc = 5 just meets the minimum","Fully acceptable, because Part-to-Part %Study Var is above 90%","Unacceptable, because repeatability is larger than reproducibility"],answer:1,hint:"Total Gage R&R %Study Var: < 10% acceptable · 10–30% marginal (may be acceptable) · > 30% unacceptable. ndc ≥ 5.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Judge the system on the **Total Gage R&R** line of %Study Var, then check the number of distinct categories."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"24.61% falls in the 10–30% band (marginal), and ndc = 5 meets the minimum of 5. The system can be used with caution, and repeatability (21%) is where improvement would help most."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Do not read the part-to-part line as the verdict, and do not expect %Study Var to add to 100%."}],final:"Total GRR 24.61% %SV, ndc = 5 → marginal → answer **B**",whyWrong:["%Study Var is based on standard deviations, which do not add; the column is not meant to sum to 100%.","","The decision is made on Total Gage R&R %Study Var (24.61%), not on the part-to-part line.","Which component is larger tells you where to improve, not whether the system is acceptable."],bookRef:"Book pp. 185–186, 190"},{id:"sv-2-182",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Gage R&R (ANOVA): Fibre-loss meter (dB)

                              %Contribution
Source              VarComp   (of VarComp)
Total Gage R&R      0.00410          13.40
  Repeatability     0.00120           3.92
  Reproducibility   0.00290           9.48
    Operator        0.00020           0.65
    Operator*Part   0.00270           8.82
Part-To-Part        0.02650          86.60
Total Variation     0.03060         100.00
\`\`\`

What is the most useful next step to improve this measurement system?`,options:["Find out why the operators measure certain fibre samples differently (for example connector cleaning or handling), because the Operator*Part interaction is the largest source","Repair or replace the meter, because repeatability is the largest source","No action is needed, because Total Gage R&R contributes only 13.4%","Retrain the operators to remove an overall difference between their averages, because the Operator term is the largest source"],answer:0,hint:"Read the largest measurement component. %Study Var = $\\sqrt{\\%Contribution}$ (as proportions): 1% ↔ 10%, 9% ↔ 30%.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In %Contribution (variances), the measurement sources add up: 3.92 + 0.65 + 8.82 = 13.40. The biggest one shows where to act."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Operator*Part (8.82%) dominates. Some operators measure **some** samples differently, so the team should study those samples and how they are handled. Also $$\\%SV=\\sqrt{0.134}=0.366$$ so the system is not acceptable as it stands."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Repeatability and the Operator term are small, and 13.4% contribution is well above the 9% (30% Study Var) limit."}],final:"Operator*Part interaction is the largest source → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Repeatability contributes only 3.92%; it is not the largest source.","A %Contribution of 13.4% is a %Study Var of about 37%, which is unacceptable.","The Operator term is only 0.65%: the operators’ overall averages hardly differ. The problem is part-specific."],trap:"13.4% Contribution looks small but is about 37% Study Var: convert before you judge.",bookRef:"Book pp. 184–186, 189–190"},{id:"sv-2-183",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
Two-Way ANOVA Table With Interaction

Source           DF       SS        MS       F      P
Part              9   2.0841   0.23157  142.94  0.000
Operator          2   0.0412   0.02060   12.72  0.000
Part * Operator  18   0.0292   0.00162    1.08  0.414
Repeatability    30   0.0450   0.00150
Total            59   2.1995
\`\`\`

Using α = 0.05, which statement about this Gage R&R is correct?`,options:["The operators’ averages differ significantly, so the operators add reproducibility error","The parts differ significantly, which shows the measurement system cannot tell the parts apart","There is a significant operator-by-part interaction","Repeatability is not significant, because it has no P-value"],answer:0,hint:"In the Gage R&R ANOVA: Part significant = good (parts are distinguishable). Operator significant = reproducibility problem. P < α → significant.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each P-value tests one source. Parts **should** be significant; operators and the interaction ideally should not be."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Operator: P = 0.000 < 0.05 → the operators’ averages differ, which adds reproducibility error. Part * Operator: P = 0.414 → not significant."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A significant Part term is good news, and repeatability is the baseline error term, not a tested factor."}],final:"Operator P = 0.000 → significant operator effect → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Significant part differences are what we **want**: they show the system can detect real differences between the parts chosen.","P = 0.414 > 0.05, so the interaction is not significant.","Repeatability is the error term that the other mean squares are compared with; it has no P-value of its own."],bookRef:"Book p. 196"},{id:"sv-2-184",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"In the graphs of a crossed Gage R&R on a call-timing tool, the **Xbar chart by operator** shows almost every point **inside** the control limits. What does this tell the Black Belt?",options:["The parts chosen for the study were too different from each other","The operators are consistent, because the ranges of their repeat readings are small","The measurement variation is large compared with the differences between the parts, so the tool cannot tell the parts apart well","The measurement system is stable and adequate, because points inside the control limits are always good"],answer:2,hint:"Gage R&R Xbar chart: limits are based on repeatability. Good system → most points OUT of control. R chart: good system → all points IN control.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Xbar chart limits in a Gage R&R are built from the repeat-measurement ranges, so they show the size of measurement error. Parts chosen to cover the process range should fall **outside** these limits."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Almost all points inside the limits means part-to-part differences are no bigger than measurement noise: the system has poor discrimination."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Do not apply the usual SPC reading (“in control = good”) to this chart, and do not confuse it with the R chart."}],final:"Xbar chart mostly in control → measurement noise swamps the parts → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Very different parts would push points **outside** the limits, which is what we want to see.","Consistency of repeat readings is read from the **R chart** by operator, not the Xbar chart.","","On this chart the limits come from measurement error, so “in control” means the part differences are hidden in the noise, which is bad here."],trap:"On a Gage R&R Xbar chart, “out of control” is the good result.",bookRef:"Book pp. 187–188"},{id:"sv-2-185",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A Gage R&R reports **Number of Distinct Categories = 1**. What does this mean?",options:["The system is ideal, because every part is measured in one consistent category","The gauge reads to one decimal place","Only one operator took part, so reproducibility could not be estimated","The system cannot tell parts apart; at most it can show whether output is conforming or not, so it is unacceptable for estimating process parameters"],answer:3,hint:"$$ndc=1.41\\times\\dfrac{\\sigma_{part}}{\\sigma_{GRR}}$$ rounded down. 1 → unacceptable · 2–4 → coarse · ≥ 5 → recommended.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The number of distinct categories tells you how many separate groups of parts the measurement system can distinguish within the process variation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"With ndc = 1 every part looks the same to the gauge. The book says this only indicates conforming versus non-conforming and is unacceptable for estimating process parameters."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"ndc is not about the number of operators or decimal places, and 1 is the worst possible result, not the best."}],final:"ndc = 1 → cannot distinguish parts → answer **D**",whyWrong:["ndc counts how many separate groups of parts the system can distinguish; 1 means none can be told apart.","ndc is not the gauge resolution, although poor resolution can be one reason for a low ndc.","ndc does not count operators; it compares part variation with measurement variation.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 185–186"},{id:"sv-2-186",phase:2,topic:"2.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"When reviewing a crossed Gage R&R, all of the following suggest an **adequate** measurement system EXCEPT:",options:["Number of distinct categories = 7","Most points on the Xbar chart by operator fall outside the control limits","Total Gage R&R %Study Var = 8%","Several points on the R chart by operator fall above the upper control limit"],answer:3,hint:"Good Gage R&R: %SV < 10%, ndc ≥ 5, Xbar chart mostly OUT of control, R chart IN control.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The R chart by operator shows the ranges of repeat readings. Points above its UCL mean some repeat readings differ far more than expected, which is a repeatability or technique problem."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The other three options (ndc 7, %SV 8% and an Xbar chart mostly out of control) are all signs of a good measurement system."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Do not mix up the two charts: out of control is good on the Xbar chart and bad on the R chart."}],final:"R chart out of control is the warning sign → answer **D**",whyWrong:["An ndc of 5 or more is recommended, so 7 is a good result.","This is the desired pattern: the parts differ by more than the measurement error.","Below 10% %Study Var is acceptable.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 186–188"},{id:"sv-2-187",phase:2,topic:"2.3.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Three analysts each review the same 50 transactions twice and flag them **Fraud** or **Not fraud**:

\`\`\`
Attribute Agreement Analysis: Fraud review

Within Appraisers
Appraiser  # Inspected  # Matched  Percent
Analyst 1          50         48    96.00
Analyst 2          50         50   100.00
Analyst 3          50         47    94.00

Each Appraiser vs Standard
Appraiser  # Inspected  # Matched  Percent   Kappa
Analyst 1          50         46    92.00    0.84
Analyst 2          50         36    72.00    0.43
Analyst 3          50         45    90.00    0.80
\`\`\`

What does Analyst 2’s result show?`,options:["Analyst 2 has a repeatability problem, giving different answers to the same transaction on the two trials","Analyst 2 is completely consistent but often disagrees with the standard, so is applying a different rule and needs to be re-aligned with the standard","Analyst 2 agrees with the standard on 43% of the transactions","Analyst 2 is the best appraiser, because within-appraiser agreement is 100%"],answer:1,hint:"Within appraiser = repeatability. Appraiser vs standard = accuracy. Kappa ≥ 0.7 acceptable (0.9 preferred).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Within-appraiser agreement shows consistency; agreement with the standard shows correctness. Kappa adjusts the agreement for chance; at least 0.7 is usually required."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Analyst 2: 100% within but only 72% against the standard, kappa 0.43 < 0.7. Analyst 2 is **consistently wrong** on some kinds of case, the classic sign of a different decision rule. Training on the standard and clearer operational definitions are needed."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Do not read 100% within-appraiser agreement as “best”, and do not read kappa as a percentage."}],final:"Consistent but disagrees with the standard → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Within-appraiser agreement is 100%, so Analyst 2 gave the same answer both times; repeatability is fine.","","Kappa is not a percentage of matches; the match rate is 72%. Kappa corrects agreement for chance.","Being consistent with yourself is not enough; agreement with the standard (72%, kappa 0.43) is poor."],bookRef:"Book pp. 201–203"},{id:"sv-2-188",phase:2,topic:"2.3.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A team plans an attribute agreement study of **Pass/Fail** decisions on installation photos. The draft plan uses **10 photos**, all clearly good or clearly bad. They are shown in the same order in each trial, and the appraisers know which photos are in the test. Which change would improve the study MOST?",options:["Keep the 10 photos but add more appraisers","Replace the expert standard with the appraisers’ majority vote","Use many more photos, include borderline cases, randomise the order of each trial and keep the test blind","Switch to a crossed variable Gage R&R on the photos"],answer:2,hint:"Attribute MSA: many samples (about 5 times a variable study), include borderline items, known standard, random order, blind trials, at least 2 trials.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says an attribute MSA needs many samples (at least five times as many as a continuous study, for example 50), should be blind, and should use randomised samples."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Ten easy photos in a fixed order that the appraisers recognise will show near-perfect agreement that is not real. Adding borderline photos tests the operational definition where it matters."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"More appraisers, majority votes or a variable study do not solve these design flaws."}],final:"More samples, borderline cases, random and blind → answer **C**",whyWrong:["More appraisers do not fix too few samples, easy-only cases or a non-blind, fixed-order test.","A majority vote can be wrong; the study needs an expert standard to judge accuracy.","","Pass/Fail judgements are attribute data; a variable Gage R&R needs measurements on a continuous scale."],bookRef:"Book pp. 199–200"},{id:"sv-2-189",phase:2,topic:"2.3.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Which order is correct for running an attribute agreement study on call-quality Pass/Fail decisions?

- **P** Each appraiser classifies all calls in random order without knowing which call is which
- **Q** An expert sets the known standard (correct answer) for every selected call
- **R** Select calls covering the full range, including borderline cases
- **S** Repeat the classification in a new random order, then compare within appraisers, between appraisers and against the standard`,options:["R → P → Q → S","R → Q → P → S","R → Q → S → P","Q → R → P → S"],answer:1,hint:"Select samples → set the standard → trial 1 (random, blind) → trial 2 (new random order) → analyse agreement and kappa.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An attribute agreement study needs chosen samples with a known correct answer before anyone is tested, then at least two blind, randomised trials."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Choose the calls (R) → expert sets the standard (Q) → first blind trial (P) → repeat and analyse (S)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Each wrong order either sets the standard on calls not yet chosen, sets it after the appraisers, or analyses before the data exist."}],final:"R → Q → P → S → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The standard should be fixed before the appraisers start, so their answers cannot influence it.","","Agreement cannot be compared before the first round of classification has been done.","The standard is set for each **selected** call, so the calls must be chosen first."],bookRef:"Book pp. 199–202"},{id:"sv-2-190",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Order-fulfilment time has **LSL = 4 days** and **USL = 12 days**. A capability study reports a sample mean of **8.6 days**, StDev(Within) = **0.8 days** and StDev(Overall) = **1.0 day**. What is **Ppk**?",options:["1.42","1.53","1.13","1.33"],answer:2,hint:"$$Ppk=\\min\\left(\\dfrac{USL-\\bar{x}}{3s_{overall}},\\ \\dfrac{\\bar{x}-LSL}{3s_{overall}}\\right)$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$LSL=4$, $USL=12$, $\\bar{x}=8.6$, $s_{overall}=1.0$ (Ppk uses the **overall** SD)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$Ppk=\\min\\left(\\dfrac{USL-\\bar{x}}{3s_{overall}},\\ \\dfrac{\\bar{x}-LSL}{3s_{overall}}\\right)$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$PPU=\\dfrac{12-8.6}{3\\times 1.0}=\\dfrac{3.4}{3}=1.13$$ and $$PPL=\\dfrac{8.6-4}{3\\times 1.0}=\\dfrac{4.6}{3}=1.53$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Ppk is the smaller value, **1.13**, set by the upper limit that the mean is closer to."}],final:"Ppk = 1.13 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This uses StDev(Within), which gives Cpk, not Ppk.","This takes the distance to the **lower** limit (the larger one); Ppk uses the nearer limit.","","This is Pp: it uses the full tolerance and ignores where the mean sits."],bookRef:"Book pp. 216–218"},{id:"sv-2-191",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Process Capability Report for Order-to-Activation Time (h)

LSL             4.00    Potential (Within)    Overall
USL            16.00    Cp    1.45            Pp    0.82
Sample Mean    10.20    Cpk   1.40            Ppk   0.79
Sample N         125
StDev(Within)  1.379
StDev(Overall) 2.439
\`\`\`

What does the large gap between Cp/Cpk and Pp/Ppk mainly indicate?`,options:["The process mean is badly off-centre","The measurement system is poor","The specification limits are too narrow","The process shifts or drifts between subgroups over time (long-term variation is much larger than short-term), so it is probably not stable"],answer:3,hint:"Cp vs Cpk gap → centring problem. Cp vs Pp gap (within SD much smaller than overall SD) → variation between subgroups over time, a stability problem.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Cp and Cpk use StDev(Within), the short-term variation inside subgroups. Pp and Ppk use StDev(Overall), which also includes shifts and drifts between subgroups."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"StDev(Overall) = 2.439 is about 1.8 times StDev(Within) = 1.379, so Pp falls from 1.45 to 0.82. The process could be capable (entitlement), but it moves over time. Check the control chart for special causes."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Centring is fine (Cp ≈ Cpk), and neither the specification nor the gauge explains a within-versus-overall gap."}],final:"Large within-to-overall gap → shifts over time → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Cp (1.45) and Cpk (1.40) are almost equal, and so are Pp and Ppk, so the process is well centred.","Measurement error inflates both the within and the overall SD; it does not explain overall SD being almost twice the within SD.","The specification limits are the same in both sets of indices, so they cannot explain the gap between them.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 211, 215–218"},{id:"sv-2-192",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Process Capability Report for Repair Visit Duration (min)

LSL 30.0   USL 90.0   Sample Mean 66.4   Sample N 40
StDev(Overall) 9.20   Anderson-Darling P-Value 0.47

Performance      Observed   Expected Overall
PPM < LSL            0.00               38.0
PPM > USL            0.00            5,155.6
PPM Total            0.00            5,193.6
\`\`\`

A supervisor says: “Observed PPM is zero, so no visit will ever exceed the specification.” What is the best response?`,options:["The expected PPM is invalid, because an AD P-value above 0.05 means the data are not normal","The process must be fully capable, because the sample mean lies between the LSL and the USL","With only 40 visits, seeing zero is likely even at about 5,000 PPM. Because the data look normal, the expected overall PPM (about 0.5% over the USL) is the better estimate of long-run performance","Agree, because observed performance is always more reliable than expected performance"],answer:2,hint:"Observed PPM = what the sample showed. Expected PPM = what the fitted (normal) model predicts. Trust the model only when the normality check is passed.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Observed performance counts the out-of-spec values in the sample. Expected performance uses the mean and SD with the normal model to predict the long-run rate."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The normality test is passed (P = 0.47), so the expected figure applies: about 5,156 PPM above the USL. At that rate the chance of 40 visits with none over 90 min is about 81%, so zero observed is unsurprising."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Observed PPM from a small sample is not proof of zero defects, and the AD rule and the tails argument are reversed or missing in the other options."}],final:"Zero observed in 40 ≠ zero risk; use expected PPM → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This reverses the rule: P = 0.47 > 0.05 means we fail to reject normality, so the normal model is reasonable.","A mean inside the limits says nothing about the tails. Here the upper tail is only about 2.6 standard deviations from the mean.","","Observed PPM can only count what happened in 40 visits, so it cannot show rates much below 1 in 40 (25,000 PPM)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 218–221"},{id:"sv-2-193",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Chat wait times are strongly right-skewed (Anderson-Darling P-value < 0.005), and the sponsor asks for Cpk from a **normal** capability analysis. What is the most appropriate response in Measure?",options:["Report Cp instead of Cpk, because Cp does not need normal data","Explain that a normal Cpk would be misleading; report observed performance (or use a non-normal capability method) and investigate why the data are skewed","Remove the longest waits until the Anderson-Darling test no longer rejects normality, then report Cpk","Report the normal Cpk anyway, because capability indices do not depend on the shape of the distribution"],answer:1,hint:"Always test normality before normal capability. Non-normal data → observed performance, or a non-normal method (for example a transformation or a fitted distribution) → then find out why.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s caveat: capability indices assume normally distributed data. In Measure, stick with observed performance unless the data are normal."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"P < 0.005 rejects normality, so a normal Cpk should not be reported. Give observed performance or use a non-normal capability method, and look at why the waits are skewed (for example queue peaks)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Ignoring the shape, deleting data, or switching to Cp all leave the wrong model in place."}],final:"Non-normal data → no normal Cpk; observed or non-normal method → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Cp uses the same normal-based 6σ spread, so it has the same problem, and it also ignores centring.","","Deleting real long waits to pass a test hides the very customer pain the project should fix.","Normal capability indices assume normal data; with a long right tail the predicted PPM can be badly wrong."],bookRef:"Book pp. 157, 221"},{id:"sv-2-194",phase:2,topic:"2.4.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"For the same process and the same within-subgroup standard deviation, when are **Cp and Cpk equal**?",options:["When the process mean is exactly at the midpoint of the specification limits","When the within and overall standard deviations are equal","When the process is in statistical control","When the process has only an upper specification limit"],answer:0,hint:"$$Cp=\\dfrac{USL-LSL}{6\\sigma}\\qquad Cpk=\\min\\left(\\dfrac{USL-\\mu}{3\\sigma},\\ \\dfrac{\\mu-LSL}{3\\sigma}\\right)$$",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Cp uses the whole tolerance and ignores the mean; Cpk uses the distance from the mean to the **nearer** limit."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only when the mean sits exactly in the middle are both one-sided distances equal to half the tolerance, so Cpk = Cp. Otherwise Cpk < Cp."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Stability, equal SDs and one-sided limits describe other relationships."}],final:"Centred process → Cp = Cpk → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Equal within and overall SDs make Cp equal to **Pp** (and Cpk equal to Ppk), not Cp equal to Cpk.","Stability is needed for capability to be meaningful, but a stable process can still be off-centre, giving Cpk < Cp.","With only one limit Cp cannot be calculated at all; only the one-sided index can."],bookRef:"Book p. 216"},{id:"sv-2-195",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A stable process shows only **common-cause** variation, but its spread is still too wide for the customer. What is needed to reduce the variation?",options:["Nothing, because common-cause variation can never be reduced","Finding and removing the single assignable cause behind each high point","Adjusting the process after every point that lands away from the mean","A fundamental change to the process (the system) itself, usually needing management action, because the variation comes from many small causes built into the process"],answer:3,hint:"Special cause → find and remove the specific cause (often local action). Common cause → change the system (usually management action).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Common-cause variation is the natural, random variation of the process as it is designed. It comes from many small causes acting together."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Because the variation is built into the system, only redesigning the system reduces it, and that usually needs management decisions and resources."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Chasing individual points is either the special-cause approach or tampering, and “nothing can be done” is false."}],final:"Common cause → change the system → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Common-cause variation can be reduced, but only by changing the process (methods, systems, training, technology).","That is how special causes are handled; in a stable process there is no single assignable cause behind each point.","Reacting to every point in a stable process is tampering, which increases the variation.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 158; Book pp. 523, 526–527"},{id:"sv-2-196",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"An I chart of daily first-response time shows **several special-cause signals**. Why is it a mistake to publish a Cpk for this process now?",options:["Special causes always make Cpk larger than Cp, so the result would be impossible","The control limits must first be set equal to the specification limits","Cpk can only be calculated from attribute data","Capability predicts future performance only for a stable, predictable process; with special causes present, the Cpk describes no single repeatable process"],answer:3,hint:"Stability first, then capability. Capability is a prediction, and only a stable (in-control) process is predictable.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A capability index is a forecast of how the process will perform. That forecast only means something when the process is in statistical control."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Special-cause signals show the process is changing; its mean and spread today may not be its mean and spread tomorrow. First find and remove the special causes, then assess capability."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other options misstate what Cpk is or how control limits are set."}],final:"Unstable process → capability not meaningful → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Cpk can never be larger than Cp for the same σ, whatever the stability.","Control limits come from the process data, never from the specification limits.","Cpk is a continuous-data index; attribute data use DPU, DPMO and Z instead.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 211, 215"},{id:"sv-2-197",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An audit of **300 customer-onboarding forms**, each with **5 opportunities** for error, finds **18 defects** spread over **12 defective forms**. What is the **DPMO**?",options:["8,000","40,000","12,000","60,000"],answer:2,hint:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{\\text{defects}}{\\text{units}\\times\\text{opportunities per unit}}\\times 10^6$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Units = 300, opportunities per unit = 5, **defects** = 18 (the 12 defective forms are a distractor)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{D}{N\\times O}\\times 10^6$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{18}{300\\times 5}\\times 10^6=\\dfrac{18}{1500}\\times 10^6=12{,}000$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 12,000 defects per million opportunities."}],final:"DPMO = 12,000 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This uses the 12 defective forms instead of the 18 defects.","This is defective forms per million forms (12 ÷ 300 × 1,000,000), which is PPM defective, not DPMO.","","This ignores the opportunities: 18 ÷ 300 is DPU, so this is DPU × 1,000,000."],trap:"Defects (18) are not defectives (12): DPMO counts defects.",bookRef:"Book pp. 223–225"},{id:"sv-2-198",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"An audit records the **number of errors on each customer invoice**; one invoice can contain several errors. The team wants a baseline attribute capability. Which analysis matches this data?",options:["Poisson capability analysis, based on defects per unit (DPU)","An attribute agreement analysis of the auditors","Binomial capability analysis, based on the proportion of defective invoices","Normal capability analysis (Cp and Cpk) of the error counts"],answer:0,hint:"Counts of defects per unit → Poisson (DPU, DPMO). Good/bad per unit (defectives) → binomial (% defective, PPM).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Attribute capability depends on what is counted: **defects** (many per unit possible) follow the Poisson model; **defectives** (pass/fail per unit) follow the binomial model."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Errors per invoice, with several allowed per invoice, are defects → Poisson capability using DPU."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Binomial would only fit if each invoice were scored simply as correct or incorrect."}],final:"Defects per unit → Poisson → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Attribute agreement checks the measurement system (the auditors), not the capability of the process.","Binomial capability is for **defectives**: each invoice is simply good or bad. Here several defects per invoice are counted.","Error counts per invoice are discrete counts, usually skewed and close to zero; normal Cp and Cpk do not fit."],trap:"Defect (an error) vs defective (a unit with one or more errors): this decides Poisson vs binomial.",bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225; Syllabus 2.4.3"},{id:"sv-2-199",phase:2,topic:"2.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"While the team works through Analyze, it wants to monitor the daily **proportion of activation orders that fail**. Daily order volume varies between 300 and 900. Which control chart fits?",options:["p chart","np chart","Xbar-R chart","c chart"],answer:0,hint:"Defectives: p chart (proportion, sample size may vary) · np chart (count, constant n). Defects: c chart (constant area) · u chart (varying area).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A failed order is a **defective** (the order either fails or it does not), and the team wants the **proportion**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Proportion defective with daily sample sizes that change → p chart (its limits adjust to each day’s n)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"np needs constant n, c and u are for defect counts, and Xbar-R is for continuous data."}],final:"Proportion defective, varying n → p chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","An np chart plots the **number** of defectives and needs a constant sample size; here the volume changes every day.","An Xbar-R chart is for continuous measurements in subgroups, not pass/fail proportions.","A c chart counts defects in a constant area of opportunity, not the proportion of defective orders."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520–521, 530"},{id:"sv-2-200",phase:2,topic:"2.4.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are good reasons to keep monitoring the baseline Y while the project moves through Analyze and Improve EXCEPT:",options:["To widen the specification limits so that the process looks capable","To have a stable reference for judging whether improvements really worked","To spot special causes early so that they can be investigated","To detect changes caused by events outside the project, such as a new vendor or a system release"],answer:0,hint:"Monitoring watches the process with control limits from the data. Specification limits come from the customer and are not changed to suit the process.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Monitoring keeps the baseline honest: it shows outside changes, special causes and later the true effect of improvements."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Widening specification limits is not a monitoring purpose. It changes the customer requirement instead of the process and would hide poor performance."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other three options are standard reasons to keep the baseline chart running."}],final:"Changing specs is not a monitoring purpose → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Without a monitored baseline the team cannot show that the improvement, and not chance, moved the Y.","Early detection of special causes is a main purpose of keeping the control chart running.","Outside events can shift the baseline; monitoring separates their effect from the project’s."],bookRef:"Book pp. 516–518, 525"}];export{e as items};
